 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Technology and uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A 30 V N-channel trench power MOSFET with low on-resistance at 4.5 V drive, low gate charge and high current capability; RoHS compliant and halogen-free. Listed applications: DC/DC conversion for computers, servers and point-of-load rails, and isolated DC/DC supplies for telecom and industrial gear."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Threshold is 1.4 to 2.2 V (1.8 V typical) at 250 µA. On-resistance is given at two drive levels, both at 20 A: 5 mΩ max (4.1 mΩ typical) at 10 V and 8.5 mΩ max (6.7 mΩ typical) at 4.5 V. Gate charge to 10 V is 15.5 nC typical, 22 nC max; to 4.5 V, 6.8 nC typical, 10 nC max. Internal gate resistance is 0.7 to 2.3 Ω. The gate is rated ±20 V, with ±100 nA max leakage there."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "On-resistance rises with junction temperature: at 10 V and 20 A it is 4.1 mΩ typical (5 mΩ max) at 25 °C and 5.6 mΩ typical (6.8 mΩ max) at 125 °C. In Figure 4, at 150 °C the 10 V curve stands near 1.46 times its 25 °C value and the 4.5 V curve near 1.38 times. In Figure 2 the 125 °C transfer curve carries more current than the 25 °C one below about 4 V of gate drive."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current and power limits",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The 30 A continuous rating at TC = 25 °C is a package limit. With the case at 100 °C the limits are 23 A and 9 W. On the 1 in² FR-4 board they are 20 A and 3 W at TA = 25 °C, 16 A and 2 W at 70 °C, the 3 W and 2 W figures resting on the t ≤ 10 s junction-to-ambient resistance. The body diode carries 28 A continuously. Figure 12 holds 23 W to 25 °C case and falls linearly to zero at 150 °C; Figure 13 holds 30 A to 25 °C case, eases to about 20 A at 130 °C, then drops to zero at 150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Avalanche and voltage spikes",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each unit is avalanche tested. Single-pulse ratings are 32 A and 26 mJ at 0.05 mH, bounded by a 150 °C junction. The drain-source voltage may spike to 36 V for 100 ns."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulsed operation",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The 120 A pulsed rating is single-pulse, bounded by a 150 °C junction. Figures 9 to 11 give safe operating area, single-pulse power and transient impedance referred to the case with the device on a large heatsink; Figures 14 and 15 give single-pulse power and transient impedance referred to ambient on the 1 in² FR-4 board. The safe operating area is a single-pulse rating."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The source-drain diode drops 0.7 V typical, 1 V max, at 1 A. Recovering from 20 A at 500 A/µs takes 12.7 ns and 17.2 nC typical."
  }
 ]
}
